            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Verdict: Don't Put Your Cold Start Budget All on Meta</h2>
<p>The most common mistake跨境独立站 sellers make at cold start is allocating 60% or more of their ad budget exclusively to Meta (Facebook/Instagram). This isn't to say Meta is ineffective—rather, this approach yields the lowest ROI at this stage. Without sufficient data to train algorithms and untested creative assets, most of that spend becomes "tuition."</p>
<p>My conclusion: <strong>60% of your cold start budget should go to Google Search and TikTok, with Meta channels capped at 40%</strong>. This isn't arbitrary. Based on data from a utility app team: they allocated 80% to Meta for three months, paying $12 CPM with just 0.8% conversion rate. After adjusting to 40% allocation, Google Shopping CPA dropped from $28 to $14, and overall ROAS improved from 1.2 to 2.6.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Key Dimensions for Budget Allocation</h2>
<h3>1. Allocate by Channel Characteristics</h3>
<p>Different channels sit at different positions in the conversion funnel, requiring distinct allocation strategies:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Google Search/Shopping</strong>: High intent from active searches. Ideal for brand-term traffic and long-tail product keywords. Budget share: 30%-40%. Focus on Shopping feed quality.</li>
<li><strong>Meta (Facebook/Instagram)</strong>: Feed ads work well for brand awareness and consideration, especially for higher AOV products with longer decision cycles. But this requires 3-5 A/B-tested creative sets before scaling.</li>
<li><strong>TikTok</strong>: Still in traffic dividend phase—CPM 30%-50% lower than Meta, though conversion paths are longer. Allocate 15%-20% for testing, focusing on short video assets and retargeting.</li>
<li><strong>Email Marketing/DM</strong>: Often overlooked, but mature DTC teams reserve 10%-15% for cart abandonment recovery and repeat purchase activation. This typically delivers 3-5x the ROI of paid ads.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Adjust by Stage Dynamically</h3>
<p>Budget allocation isn't "set and forget":</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Testing Phase (0-3 months)</strong>: 70% to multi-channel testing, running 2-3 creative sets per channel simultaneously. Goal: quickly identify ROI-positive combinations.</li>
<li><strong>Scaling Phase (3-6 months)</strong>: Shift 80% to validated high-performing channels, maintain 20% for continuous testing of new creatives and audiences.</li>
<li><strong>Stabilization Phase (6+ months)</strong>: Focus shifts to lowering CAC and boosting LTV. Budget can gradually tilt toward retargeting and email, with paid acquisition dropping below 50%.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Iterate Rapidly Based on Data</h3>
<p>Many advertisers set a budget plan and don't adjust for a week. In practice, <strong>review CPA and ROAS every 72 hours</strong>—any channel underperforming by 20%+ should be paused immediately, with budget shifted to高效渠道. This isn't advanced strategy, but teams that execute it consistently versus those adjusting weekly see 40% lower CAC after six months.</p>
<hr />
<h2>5 Signs Your Budget Is Being Wasted</h2>
<p>Not sure if your allocation is optimal? Check for these signals:</p>
<ol>
<li>Meta running 2 weeks with CPM over $10 but conversion rate below 1%</li>
<li>Channels with daily budgets under $50 showing data volatility too high to interpret</li>
<li>Any channel maintaining ROAS below 1.5 for 3+ consecutive days without adjustment</li>
<li>Zero budget allocated to retargeting—all spend on new customer acquisition</li>
<li>Never tested TikTok or other Google products (like Pmax)</li>
</ol>
<p>If 2 or more apply, consider re-running channel testing and budget allocation.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Different Team Sizes Should Reference</h2>
<p>Allocation ratios aren't absolute—adjust based on team capacity and account scale:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Under $7K monthly budget</strong>: Focus on 1-2 channels. Recommend Google Search + 1 Meta channel at 50/50 during testing.</li>
<li><strong>$7K-$28K monthly</strong>: Test across 3 channels with clear priorities. Google 40%, Meta 35%, TikTok 15%, others 10%.</li>
<li><strong>$28K+ monthly</strong>: Resources for full-channel coverage, but core focus remains identifying高效渠道 and scaling quickly. Keep testing budget under 15%.</li>
</ul>
<p>One final reminder: <strong>Budget allocation is dynamic, not a monthly planning exercise</strong>. Set aside 30 minutes weekly for data review and adjust based on last week's CPA, ROAS, and channel mix. The difference between teams that do this consistently versus those that don't becomes very apparent after six months.</p>
